WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.440
- She is a chart topping
musician who also was one

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:06.270
of TIME Magazine's Most
Influential People of 2020.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.877
She's the author of the New
York Times bestselling book,

00:00:08.877 --> 00:00:12.470
"I Would Leave Me if I Could",
which is an amazing title.

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:15.150
And her new line of makeup
is called About-Face,

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:16.770
also an amazing, she's good with titles

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:17.680
and writing, people.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.240
It's available right now.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:21.487
Let's everybody say hello to Halsey!

00:00:22.529 --> 00:00:26.381
(upbeat music)
(applause)

00:00:26.381 --> 00:00:28.798
I am such a big fan of yours!

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:32.460
I think that you are
so incredibly talented.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.130
I know I'm supposed to be like host person

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.370
but right now I'm just like artist person.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:37.710
You're so incredible.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.980
Thank you so much for doing our show.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:41.710
- My gosh, I'm so excited to be here

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:43.420
and I'm so excited to be talking to you.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:46.880
You know, I went and saw
your show at Staples Center.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:48.540
I think it was one of the last concerts

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.000
that I went to and it was so good.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:51.833
- Oh my gosh.

00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:52.968
Thank you, I know.

00:00:52.968 --> 00:00:53.862
I keep making that joke.

00:00:53.862 --> 00:00:56.193
We didn't know that we had
our last like farewell tour.

00:00:56.193 --> 00:00:58.680
We didn't know we were all
on our own farewell tour.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.670
'Cause it's, yeah, no one's performing.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:02.770
Well, you're an incredible artist.

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:04.320
Have you been, not just singer,

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:05.739
I mean as in painter as well.

00:01:05.739 --> 00:01:07.890
You're kind of an all around artist.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.160
So have you been painting
while stuck at home?

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:11.760
- Yeah, I have been.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.550
I'm super guilty of starting things

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:15.150
and not finishing them ever.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.703
So I have like a bunch of half
made paintings in the house,

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:21.940
but I do have one that I just finished.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:24.320
I did it for a friend as a gift.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.970
It's a picture of their daughter

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:27.868
and they haven't seen it yet.

00:01:27.868 --> 00:01:28.760
So I figured why not let them see it

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.530
for the first time on the
"Kelly Clarkson Show"?

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:31.917
- Oh my gosh.

00:01:31.917 --> 00:01:33.174
I wanna see it!

00:01:33.174 --> 00:01:34.257
This is amazing.

00:01:34.257 --> 00:01:36.038
What a beautiful, thoughtful gift.

00:01:36.038 --> 00:01:37.533
- Okay, so here it is.

00:01:38.374 --> 00:01:40.833
(applause)
- Are you serious?

00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:44.580
- I can't hold it straight, I don't know,

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:45.530
but can you see it?

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.753
- I'm usually not speechless ever.

00:01:50.753 --> 00:01:53.510
That's really great, Halsey.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:56.331
- Thank you, thank you so much.

00:01:56.331 --> 00:01:58.310
It's like a hobby.

00:01:58.310 --> 00:01:59.291
- That's a hobby.

00:01:59.291 --> 00:02:00.960
(laughter)

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.940
You can be a professional, that's amazing,

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:05.170
it's your hobby.

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:06.510
And just from, I mean,

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:08.330
a mama's point of view,
there's nothing cooler.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:10.130
I actually just received
a really cool gift

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.780
from my friend Wise standing
here that was of my kids.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.240
There's nothing cooler
than doing something

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:15.293
like that for a mama.

00:02:16.753 --> 00:02:17.990
Like, we love our little ones so much.

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:19.780
And you're incredible.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:22.750
And another thing, wait,
I have to say this though,

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:26.180
side note, is another
thing that's been going

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.550
on while you're at home is snacks, right?

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:30.310
You're really into like
pretty random snacks.

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:31.570
- I can't stop eating.

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:33.346
I don't know what's gotten into me.

00:02:33.346 --> 00:02:34.670
You know, I think it's
like kind of being home

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:36.449
in the quarantine.

00:02:36.449 --> 00:02:39.980
I usually eat really
healthy when I'm on tour

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.171
and you know how this is,
I was on tour for six years

00:02:42.171 --> 00:02:45.570
and all I was eating was
like grilled chicken breast

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:47.403
and rice and spinach.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.900
And sometimes like greenroom gummy bears.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:54.780
But I, since I've been home, I've had time

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.240
to like experiment with
different snacks and I

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:58.840
take it pretty seriously, yeah.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.172
- I love it.

00:03:00.172 --> 00:03:01.550
Well, maybe you can explain
some of these snacks

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:02.490
you've tweeted about.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:06.270
This one is, "Dry Oreos
are so weird to me.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:07.360
Need dunkage."

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:08.876
Which I totally get.

00:03:08.876 --> 00:03:12.170
Like I have to, I like a
cookie soaked, I'm with you.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:14.277
I'm like, let's just soak
it a little bit, yeah.

00:03:14.277 --> 00:03:15.960
- I can't eat them dry.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.199
I don't know.

00:03:17.199 --> 00:03:18.100
I don't understand
people who eat Oreos dry.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:19.860
They also have to be double stuffed,

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:21.680
that's a rule for me.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.117
And sometimes I like to
smear peanut butter on them,

00:03:26.117 --> 00:03:28.120
which is an old trick I learned

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:31.940
from watching "The Parent
Trap" when I was little,

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:33.300
a young child.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.450
- Yes, oh my gosh, I love that movie.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:38.253
Also, I would really love your metabolism.

00:03:39.699 --> 00:03:40.930
I'm like, wait, what?

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:42.220
She's like, "double stuffed".

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:44.260
I'm like, oh, okay.

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:46.460
What's your latest snack obsession?

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:48.590
- I started making Rice Krispie treats,

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:50.370
but like without the Rice Krispies.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:51.540
And I know that doesn't make any sense,

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:52.960
but I make them with other cereals.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:54.720
So like Fruity Pebble treats or

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.416
like Cinnamon Toast Crunch treats,

00:03:57.416 --> 00:04:00.420
and they're so easy to
make and they're so good,

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:01.731
so good.
- That's fun to do

00:04:01.731 --> 00:04:03.230
with your kids.

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:04.230
- Yeah, definitely.

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:05.690
Oh my gosh.

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:08.250
You know, if I didn't live alone

00:04:09.385 --> 00:04:11.520
with my dog and you know,

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:16.075
have no like family or love in my home,

00:04:16.075 --> 00:04:18.137
I would probably do it with my kids too.

00:04:20.041 --> 00:04:21.858
- I love you so much.

00:04:21.858 --> 00:04:24.900
- If any of my real friends
are listening to that,

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:26.353
it was a joke, I swear.

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:30.077
- They were like, we need
to call her right now.

00:04:30.077 --> 00:04:31.747
I hate that when I make
jokes, people like,

00:04:31.747 --> 00:04:33.239
"You're fine, right?"

00:04:33.239 --> 00:04:34.375
And I'm like, "I'm just being funny."

00:04:34.375 --> 00:04:36.310
All right, so you grew up in New Jersey.

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:39.015
Is it true you guys had
a bunch of urban legends?

00:04:39.015 --> 00:04:41.050
- Yeah, Jersey is a weird place.

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:42.600
I think it's 'cause everyone's so bored

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:44.020
and we're not as cool as New York.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:46.230
So like you just kind of have to look

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.650
for things to make you special.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:49.690
So one of the things that we came

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:53.603
up with is like the Jersey
Devil who's like this,

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:58.573
I guess, creature that
lives in the forest.

00:04:58.573 --> 00:05:01.757
There's like all these really weird

00:05:01.757 --> 00:05:04.620
like phenomenon that are
really just a product

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.170
of, I think, crazy people,

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:09.800
but was like the profanity
houses and Jenny jump

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:12.350
and just like a lot of urban
legends that are mostly

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:16.000
meant to scare kids into like,
not being out past curfew,

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.160
but they definitely scared
me when I was growing up.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:20.140
I fully bought into them.

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:22.500
There's a whole book
about it called "Weird NJ"

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.830
that talks all about
how weird New Jersey is

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:26.573
with stuff like that.

00:05:26.573 --> 00:05:27.753
It's really weird.

00:05:27.753 --> 00:05:30.130
- I'm 38 and I'm not, yeah,
I won't go near that stuff.

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:32.000
So yeah, I don't even do haunted houses.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.320
I'm like, but mostly because
I don't wanna be sued,

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.150
'cause I full on will punch people.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.320
Like they'll come out at me
and I'll just be like, what?

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:40.510
And I'm just like, I get really, I don't-

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:42.274
- I believe that.

00:05:42.274 --> 00:05:43.543
- Yeah, it's not my fault.
- I fully believe with you.

00:05:44.493 --> 00:05:45.990
I fully believe that you would do that.

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:48.590
- I'm telling you, I
don't like to be scared.

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:50.700
Like my ex always thought it was so funny.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:53.350
He'd just like jumped out
and like, and I'd scream

00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:55.250
and I like lose my voice and I'm like,

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:58.340
no one's laughing, no
one thinks that's funny.

00:05:58.340 --> 00:05:59.335
People do.

00:05:59.335 --> 00:06:01.260
- I'm the jumper.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:02.690
- Oh my gosh.

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:04.194
You like to scare people?

00:06:04.194 --> 00:06:05.777
I hate that.
- Yeah, I'm the scarer

00:06:05.777 --> 00:06:07.533
in the relationship.

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:08.750
- I hate that.

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:09.990
I totally, I'm like,

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:12.290
I'm gonna pull cuddle
time if it happens again.

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:13.837
Like I was like, but anyway,

00:06:13.837 --> 00:06:16.920
I need to ask you about
this photo that you posted.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:17.840
Here we go.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.730
What's happening here?

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:21.129
What?

00:06:21.129 --> 00:06:24.150
- Okay, you should know better than anyone

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:29.020
that being on stage, you
get like random afflictions.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:30.480
You don't know where they come from.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:32.323
- Well not that big!

00:06:33.465 --> 00:06:34.471
What?

00:06:34.471 --> 00:06:37.610
- Whenever I'm on stage, I
just get this uncontrollable

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:39.930
urge to like throw myself to the ground.

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:40.850
I don't know why.

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:42.780
I just like, I'm always like falling

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:45.590
to my knees dramatically
or like crawling around

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:49.060
on my elbows and knees and
like jumping off of things.

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:53.220
And I always end up covered in
like really serious bruises.

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:55.560
The funny thing about
that picture actually is

00:06:55.560 --> 00:07:00.040
that one of the bruises has
like a fish scale shape.

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:03.474
And it's because I was bruised
through my fishnet stockings.

00:07:03.474 --> 00:07:05.560
- Oh my gosh.
- You can kind of see

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:06.640
on my knee.

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:08.130
- Wait, is that a tattoo
on your knee though?

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:09.190
Did you get a tattoo?

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:10.140
Are they band-aids?

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:11.700
- Yeah, so I got a tattoo with band-aids

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:13.680
because my knees are
always bruised and cut up

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:16.040
and it says "Poor thing",
which is kind of sarcastic,

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:18.390
'cause it's like poor thing
because my knees are always

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:20.280
bruised, but they're
bruised because I'm doing

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:23.173
the thing that I love,
you know what I mean?

00:07:23.173 --> 00:07:25.160
But it was kind of hilarious

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:28.880
and ironic because the tattoo hurt worse

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:33.037
than any stage bruise I ever got.

00:07:33.037 --> 00:07:34.658
I mean, getting a tattoo on your kneecap

00:07:34.658 --> 00:07:36.660
is exactly as bad as it sounds.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.170
- Yeah, 'cause it's thin
there, that skin is so thin.

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:43.333
Ooh, also right here is like
pretty painful, right here.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:46.633
Yeah, I was like gonna punch that guy.

00:07:47.658 --> 00:07:48.967
I was like, "You finish!"

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:51.710
Well your mom actually took you to get

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:53.060
your first tattoo, right?

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:54.110
- Yeah, she did.

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:55.280
My mom's like my best friend.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:56.390
We did everything together.

00:07:56.390 --> 00:07:58.560
I got my first tattoo, it's on my foot.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.500
I got her initials inside
an anchor and she got mine.

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:03.620
I was 16.

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:04.750
And she was like, oh gosh,

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:08.130
I hope this doesn't turn into
like a reoccurring problem.

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:10.880
10 years later I have like 70 tattoos.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:13.618
So, you know, you could
say it's her fault.

00:08:13.618 --> 00:08:15.800
(laughter)

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:17.340
You know, I just fell in love with them.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:18.620
I just loved getting them.

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:20.882
And they're cool 'cause
they're kind of like

00:08:20.882 --> 00:08:21.715
a physical passport stamp.

00:08:21.715 --> 00:08:23.930
Like I get tattoos all over the world.

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:27.890
So I have ones from like London
and Russia and Australia.

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:30.610
And it's like, it's cool to
remember when I got them.

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:32.130
- Yeah, no, I love that too.

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:33.360
I have like around 17,

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:34.430
nowhere near as many as you,

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:36.160
but I love them because
they are all memories

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:38.420
of like different chapters
in my life as well

00:08:38.420 --> 00:08:40.200
and they all mean something.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.160
But what's been the most random

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:44.642
and inspiration for a
song that you've written?

00:08:44.642 --> 00:08:48.654
- Ooh, there was a song on my second album

00:08:48.654 --> 00:08:51.500
called "Bad at Love".

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:56.500
And the song is about me failing miserably

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:58.200
at like a bunch of relationships.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:08:59.307
That's what it's about.

00:08:59.307 --> 00:09:01.840
But the inspiration for writing it was,

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:03.300
I've always wanted a motorcycle

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:07.230
and I'm way too scared to ride
one and I'll never have one.

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:09.620
I just always picture myself getting to.

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:11.690
And so when I was making
the album, I was like,

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:14.670
I want a music video
where I'm on a motorcycle.

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:16.960
I was like, and I want the
song to sound like a song

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:20.070
you'd be like cruising down the road

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:22.680
on your motorcycle listening to

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:25.580
and just like with your
hands in the air, just like

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:29.130
and somehow that turned
into "Bad at Love".

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:31.860
It doesn't make any sense at all, but-

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:33.250
- It does!
- It was inspiration

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:34.870
for the sound of it.

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:36.906
- No, I'm like that almost
whenever I write a song as well,

00:09:36.906 --> 00:09:38.790
I have to have imagery.

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:40.020
I almost have a video playing

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:42.460
while I'm writing in my head.

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:44.520
How do you deal, like when you're writing,

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:45.670
how do you deal with writer's block?

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:48.522
If you're like needing to
cut, like you're working

00:09:48.522 --> 00:09:50.026
on an album for whatever reason,
you're just like blocked.

00:09:50.026 --> 00:09:50.859
How do you do that?

00:09:50.859 --> 00:09:52.120
- Oh, usually it's by doing something

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:54.450
like super regrettable, like cutting bangs

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:56.426
or piercing my nose.

00:09:56.426 --> 00:09:58.280
(laughing)

00:09:58.280 --> 00:09:59.280
- Super regrettable.

00:10:01.528 --> 00:10:04.150
- Usually that's what I do.

00:10:04.150 --> 00:10:08.959
There's three things I do,
I dye my hair or cut bangs,

00:10:08.959 --> 00:10:13.616
I get a tattoo or I
call an ex for closure.

00:10:13.616 --> 00:10:16.420
(laughter)

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:19.541
Two of the things never
worked out well, so you know,

00:10:19.541 --> 00:10:20.690
I don't know.

00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:22.350
- I loved you before.

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:23.970
I love you even more now.

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:26.030
I've heard people say this about you,

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:26.930
I've heard you say this about you,

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:28.200
that you're kind of an outsider

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:30.159
and you have been most of your life.

00:10:30.159 --> 00:10:31.470
So was it incredible to be named one

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:35.320
of TIME Magazines Most
Influential People of 2020?

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:36.153
- Oh my gosh.

00:10:36.153 --> 00:10:38.980
I was so, so surprised, first of all.

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:42.990
Also, here's a really
funny story about that.

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:45.270
So I told my parents that I was gonna be

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:48.218
on the cover of TIME Magazine
and I like built it up

00:10:48.218 --> 00:10:50.420
to be this like huge announcement.

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:52.170
'Cause like, I love my parents,

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:55.390
but they're never excited
about anything I do anymore.

00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:56.950
There was just like, oh cool.

00:10:56.950 --> 00:10:58.498
You know what I mean?

00:10:58.498 --> 00:11:00.420
I could be like, performing
at The White House,

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:04.100
you know, and they'd be like, "Great."

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:05.461
You know, I'd be like,

00:11:05.461 --> 00:11:06.390
"I got a Nobel Peace Prize."

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:08.400
And they'd be like, "That's cool, Ash."

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:10.920
And so I told them that I was gonna be

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:14.020
on the cover of TIME Magazine and my dad

00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:15.917
sent me a thumbs up emoji

00:11:15.917 --> 00:11:18.257
and my mom sent me a text that said,

00:11:18.257 --> 00:11:20.543
"Wow, super cool, chick."

00:11:20.543 --> 00:11:23.200
(laughter)
(applause)

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:24.350
- I actually love that.

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:27.642
It's like a great way to keep you humble.

00:11:27.642 --> 00:11:29.740
- To make you proud of me.

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:32.620
Like what do I need do?

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:34.290
So in case you guys are wondering why

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:37.300
I'm an overachiever it's
like, hopefully my parents

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:39.810
at that situation explains it.

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:42.320
- I know, but I think it's
kind of an amazing thing though

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:43.760
because I think they kind of come

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:46.460
across as parents that
are proud of you already,

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:48.970
like in general of like any
small thing you might do.

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:51.100
So it's kind of awesome to be like, okay,

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:53.700
that was not impressive, cool.

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:55.962
That's totally happened to me as well.

00:11:55.962 --> 00:11:58.010
And it's always the things you think

00:11:58.010 --> 00:11:59.160
that are gonna be amazing.

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:00.800
And you're like, oh my God, I get to...

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:02.150
And then the things you're like, yeah,

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:04.280
this happened and people
are like, are you serious?

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:06.660
And I'm like, whatever,
I don't even try anymore.

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:09.701
But you have a new makeup
line, which I actually,

00:12:09.701 --> 00:12:13.830
I'm not super into makeup,
but I love how you do yours.

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:16.670
And every performance I
see, like on award shows

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:19.320
or on "The Voice", you came
in and did this really cool

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:21.480
thing, you are just amazing at makeup.

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:24.042
So it's called About-Face, right?

00:12:24.042 --> 00:12:25.180
- Yes it is.

00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:27.260
It's called About-Face and that's

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:29.590
for two reasons, well three.

00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:32.700
First is 'cause it's about
face, obviously, it's makeup.

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:35.260
Second reason is because
an about face is to

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:37.040
make a complete turn in direction,

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:38.520
which I thought was really cool.

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:40.300
And it's kind of like a militant phrase.

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:42.080
And I always feel like
I'm like going into battle

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:43.730
when I'm putting on my makeup.

00:12:43.730 --> 00:12:45.370
But the main reason it's cool

00:12:45.370 --> 00:12:48.230
is because AF are my initials.

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:52.030
I am Ashley Frangipane,
so all the makeup says AF

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:53.423
and that's me.

00:12:53.423 --> 00:12:54.640
- I'm like a person that loves that too.

00:12:54.640 --> 00:12:56.619
I love meaning in everything
or there's no reason for it.

00:12:56.619 --> 00:12:58.610
Where can people get it?

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:00.390
- You can get it at about-face.com,

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:01.970
super, super easy.

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:04.870
Our Instagram is About-FaceBeauty
and it's super fun.

00:13:04.870 --> 00:13:06.150
There's a lot of really cool photos

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:08.540
on there where you can
get some like makeup inspo

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:11.930
and everything's vegan and
clean and cruelty-free,

00:13:11.930 --> 00:13:14.070
so, you know it's very, very good for you

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:15.770
and good for the planet.

00:13:15.770 --> 00:13:16.810
- That's awesome.

00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:18.270
All right, well, before we go to break,

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:21.381
can you tell us about
your Black Creators Fund?

00:13:21.381 --> 00:13:23.390
- Yeah, of course I would love to.

00:13:23.390 --> 00:13:24.800
The Black Creators Funding Initiative

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:28.590
we started right at the
beginning of the quarantine.

00:13:28.590 --> 00:13:30.870
It's something I've wanted
to do for a really long time.

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:34.480
And you know, we kind of
just got a little kick

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:36.199
to get it done faster this year.

00:13:36.199 --> 00:13:38.900
I really wanted to put
some stuff on my timeline

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:40.230
that was really positive.

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:43.160
I felt like a lot of my
followers who were black

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:45.810
indigenous people of color
were seeing like a lot

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:48.080
of negativity and a lot of violence.

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:51.290
And I wanted them to
see something positive

00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:53.203
on their social media,

00:13:53.203 --> 00:13:56.860
to kind of lighten the mood

00:13:56.860 --> 00:14:00.430
and help them celebrate their community.

00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:04.050
So we started a project
where we give grants

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:08.190
to black creatives and people who,

00:14:08.190 --> 00:14:10.120
it's everything from makeup artists

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:14.877
to photographers, directors,
clothing designers, poets.

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:19.790
I mean, the crop of
talent is unbelievable.

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:23.730
And so many of them have had
such an incredible start.

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:26.630
There's a band, "Meet Me By
The Altar" that we selected

00:14:28.352 --> 00:14:29.460
in, I think, the second round

00:14:29.460 --> 00:14:32.290
and they just signed a record deal.

00:14:32.290 --> 00:14:34.500
There was a fashion designer named Asada

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:36.540
from the first round and
she just had her stuff

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:38.470
featured in "Vogue".
- Wow!

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:41.110
- And it's just really
cool flourishing like that.

00:14:41.110 --> 00:14:44.050
And a lot of these people
are incredible creatives

00:14:44.050 --> 00:14:48.510
who don't get jobs as often

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:50.310
as their white counterparts.

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:53.490
And so felt important to
us to create a platform

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:57.040
where our peers in the industry
would have an opportunity

00:14:57.040 --> 00:15:00.560
to hire from this crop of
creatives before they defaulted

00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:03.740
to the usual suspects
on the list, you know?

00:15:03.740 --> 00:15:06.050
- Yeah and just make sure
everybody has the opportunity

00:15:06.050 --> 00:15:07.930
and everybody to a shot.

00:15:07.930 --> 00:15:09.630
That that's what it's about.

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:11.630
Good luck with your makeup line.

00:15:11.630 --> 00:15:14.022
And y'all, Halsey's new
makeup line is called

00:15:14.022 --> 00:15:15.510
About-Face and y'all
be sure to look for it.

00:15:15.510 --> 00:15:17.099
And I mean,

00:15:17.099 --> 00:15:17.932
you know what you should do?

00:15:17.932 --> 00:15:19.410
You should put pictures of you on stage

00:15:19.410 --> 00:15:20.440
'cause you do all these cool things

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:22.480
and have people try and
match it or do something.

00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:23.960
I'm just saying, as a little thing,

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:25.390
'cause you're amazing at it.

00:15:25.390 --> 00:15:27.470
And if you impress someone
that doesn't like makeup,

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:28.720
that's pretty impressive.

